One aspect of the proposed Treaty that will impact on employee welfare and morale is the provision for discontinuance of the hospitals, medical services and schools which the Canal Zone Government has provided for its employees. U.S. citizen employees and their dependents can use U.S. military health and educational facilities after the Treaty comes into effect, so that the greatest impact of this action will be on the non-American employees, who will have to use Panamanian facilities.
In sum, actions under the Panama Canal Treaty will impact on the employment and other economic and social aspects of the human environment in the Canal Zone; there are Treaty provisons and plans to implement them which seek to ameliorate the adverse effects of these actions.
4. Shedding of Canal Company Zone Service and Commercial Operations
Annex B of the Agreement in Implementation of Article III of the proposed treaty stipulates that the Panama Canal Company/Canal Zone Government will discontinue the following commercial activities when the Treaty becomes effective:
 wholesale and retail stores;
 facilities for the production of food and drinks;
 public restaurants and cafeterias;
 recreational & amusement facilities;
 laundry & dry cleaning plants;
 service & repair facilities for private automobiles, appliances, boats, motors, furniture, etc;
cold storage & freezer plants;
 freight houses;
 commercial services to private vessels;
 printing services;
 maritime transport for the public;
 health & medical services to individuals;
 educational services to individuals;
 postal services; and
 immigration, customs & quarantine controls not necessary for the sanitation of the Canal.
The police, court and prison system of the Canal Zone Government is to be discontinued by the new Panama Canal Commission within 30 months from the entry of the Treaty in force. Transition provisions
